and covet are both  peaking at the same time  what can be done about this should kids  be wearing masks can there be an  increased push for vaccinations  we know that  the influenza a  virus that's currently circulating  is  one that's not ideally matched to the  vaccine that that was used this season  so when that happens while vaccine can  still help somewhat  we know that in the past the  effectiveness of influenza vaccine kind  of ranges year to year  what i would say with respect to the  things that are most important to  manage or lower the risk for both  influenza and kovid whether it's for  kids or people of any other age  is  again making sure that hands are washed  regularly certainly masks are an  important intervention and especially  for those who have risk factors wearing  masks in public places is an important  consideration  also making sure that  we continue to normalize in all of our  circles  the important habit of staying home when  sick so  trying to avoid uh being around people  who have a respiratory illness  and making sure that  we're supporting those who who need  perhaps a little bit of time  if they're feeling sick to to not be in  close proximity to others so those are  really the key things obviously covid  vaccination is highly effective at  preventing severe outcomes so that's an  important intervention for children who  are old enough to receive that  and for younger children again it really  is about those other key preventive  measures that people can take every day  thanks dr hinshaw we'll go to the next  caller please  terry ctv  hi there uh i just have a quick question  about the situation in calgary in terms  of the wastewater to the minister or dr  hinshaw i know that we're saying in  other areas that declining what is the  situation like in calgary is it you know  staying the same or increasing and just  what kind of concerning levels are those  at any kind of reminders to calgarians  or anything of that note  i'll maybe start and and then if the  minister wants to add uh that would be  great so  we are seeing uh you have seen  throughout the last month or so higher  levels in calgary with respect to virus  and wastewater  we know that that's a marker of  transmission in the community that  doesn't rely on test seeking behaviors  but  we are you know again seeing that in  most other places in the province that  decline from a previous peak we expect  that in calgary  we would start to see that as well we  know our other leading indicator of test  positivity is beginning to decline and  so  ultimately i think the the  important thing for albertans to be able  to do when they're wondering about risk  in their own community is to look at the  information that's available on the  alberta health website  where they can see multiple indicators  including the cases that have been  diagnosed in the last week in in their  geographic area they can see the  positivity rates in different zones and  they can see the wastewater results for  different cities where that's being  tracked and use that as a measure of  what the risk might be in those  communities to inform the decisions that  they make about their daily activities  thanks dr hinshaw terry follow-up  yeah just on that note i should have  asked at the beginning but um is there  any uh indication why calgary's kind of  bucking the trend  it's really difficult to say one of the  things that's important to  remember about wastewater again is that  the absolute level is difficult to  compare from one city to another just  because there's some different sampling  techniques so while we're working on  variation uh reducing variation across  the province what is most important is  to look at the the trends in any given  area so it's really difficult to say why  calgary might be a little bit different  why they might  not be coming down as as quickly as  other places but ultimately whatever the  the reasons for that change in the or  the difference in the trend again the  important thing is to use that as one of  several metrics when people are looking  at what their risks might be in their  community to inform those uh protective  actions that they take  thanks dr hinshaw we started with two  but i think one more joint so operator  we'll take a third call and then stop  there  laura beamish fort mac today  thank you so much for taking my question  minister copping um it's unrelated to  covid but back in 2018 it was reported  the ndp government was discussing  expanding abortion services to fort  mcmurray left bridge medicine hat grand  prairie and red beer obviously kovid 19  has had a great impact since then but is  pursuing this a priority  so so my understanding um when uh my  predecessor was asked the same question  and looked into that is that  you know even though the previous  government may have spoken to that issue  you know ahs is the one that that makes  the decisions of what services will be  provided and where based on the demand  and they had no plans to be able to uh  they had no plans to increase the  uh the current level of service that  abortions are provided and as you know  abortions are provided in uh you know  those clinics in uh in calgary and  edmonton uh my understanding is hs has  no current plans to uh to expand that  but there you know also is you know  other options uh associated with uh  um  uh  uh  uh  sorry uh  uh mifo j  jimiso um which is a which is an oral  based option that is available across  the province  thanks minister laura follow up  yeah thank you minister cupping just  when you're looking at expanding and  increasing more health care support in  rural areas  isn't abortion services something that  you're talking about or looking at  so so again ahs is you know when we look  at services generally um you look at the  demand that and the requirement and then  ahs looks at that with the demand where  it's needed and how and they do that not  with just abortion they do that with  with all services um so you know uh  transfusion services for example you  know cardiac services so there is an  assessment that they actually go through  um you know one of our focuses um in the  challenges that we're having right now  is about ensuring that we have the staff  in rural alberta to be able to deliver  the services that ahs has already  determined that that should be delivered  giving the demographics and the demand  in those locations  and so we are focused on on increasing  increasing staff recruitment and  retention of doctors and other  healthcare professionals and rural  alberta to be able to deliver those  services that have already been  determined that that are are required uh  given the demographics and given the  demand  thanks minister uh and thanks dr hinshaw  uh we'll stop there and see you back  here next week for the next update  thank you
 

